{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/docset at exact clean pushed head 707b228f734a7e19c5e4e0aa15a7ac2d74211b4a. Companion to the how-to artifact b9a3386c, which is the implementation report for codex's request 47cd3672. No live predecessor at this path, so this publication introduces one rather than succeeding one.\n\nWHAT THIS ADDS. cleanup_guard_test.go, a gate that runs one documented block the way a reader runs it. Every other gate in this package executes documentation through runScript, which writes \"set -euo pipefail\" ahead of the extracted code. That preamble is right for the gate — an ordered walkthrough should stop at its first failure — but it means the package could not, before now, tell the difference between a block whose safety comes from the page and a block whose safety comes from the harness. The cloned-repo removal block was the second kind while claiming to be the first.\n\nHOW IT PROVES SOMETHING RATHER THAN PASSING. The test extracts the block by a marker rather than by line number, writes it to a script with no preamble at all, and runs it against a stub git that reports a common directory somewhere other than the copy. That is the substitution failure the two exact-path comparisons exist to catch, and the decoy directory it points at holds a sentinel file. The block must fail and the sentinel must survive.\n\nThen it runs the same extracted block a second time with its set -e line removed, and requires the sentinel to be destroyed. That second run is the part that matters. Without it, the first assertion would pass just as happily against a fixture in which nothing was ever going to be deleted, and the gate would be decoration proving a deletion is prevented in a scenario containing no deletion. Requiring the destruction is how the fixture demonstrates it has teeth.\n\nThe structural half of the check looks for a line that is exactly set -e, not for the string anywhere in the block. The comment in the page names set -e in order to explain it, and matching that would let the explanation stand in for the thing explained.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and observed rather than asserted. Removing the set -e line from the page fails the gate with \"a failed path guard did not stop the block - the decoy file was deleted\", and the sentinel was genuinely gone. Changing it to set +e fails with \"the removal block has no `set -e` line\". Restoring the page passes. Both mutations were applied to the page, not to the test, so what they exercise is the documented behaviour and not the assertion's own plumbing.\n\nWHAT THIS GATE DOES NOT DO, stated because a reader of this package should not over-read it. It covers one named block on one page. It is not a general facility for running any documented block bare, and it does not change runScript or the preamble any other gate depends on. If a second page ever needs the same treatment, the honest move is to generalise this deliberately with its own request rather than to widen the marker.\n\nGATES: covered by the module-wide run reported at b9a3386c - go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 32 packages ok - and by go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 ok at 73.260s uncached.","body":{"commit":"707b228f734a7e19c5e4e0aa15a7ac2d74211b4a","path":"internal/docset"}}